## 2. Mode normalization and the initial rate ratio

On the finite positive-frequency transverse space, with harmonic winding modes removed, use the named real orthonormal modes \(f_r\), eigenvalues \(\lambda_r>0\), and

\[
d_r=\frac{c_p\cdot f_r}{\sqrt{2\sqrt{\lambda_r}}},\quad
v=\sum_r d_r^2>0,\quad
|\alpha\rangle=\sum_r\alpha_ra_r^*|0\rangle,\quad
\sum_r|\alpha_r|^2=1,\quad \chi=\sum_r d_r\alpha_r .
\tag{3}
\]

At a different reference preparation time, the phases \(e^{-i\omega_rs}\) can be included in \(\alpha_r\) and leave its norm unchanged. This observation makes no claim that the prepared charged sector follows the old reference orbit after that time.

Cauchy–Schwarz, with the exact normalization in (3), gives

\[
0\le|\chi|^2\le v,\qquad s_\alpha:=|\chi|^2/v\in[0,1].
\tag{4}
\]

The upper endpoint is attainable in this finite reference class by \(\alpha_r=d_r/\sqrt v\), up to a common phase. A vector orthogonal to \(d\) gives the lower endpoint; the specified cubic graph has more than one positive mode. If the packet is additionally constrained to a mode subspace \(S\), replace \(v\) in the overlap upper bound by \(\|P_Sd\|^2\). Thus the bound can sharpen to \(1+2\|P_Sd\|^2/v\). The full-space bright vector is not asserted to be a narrow, low-energy astronomical packet.

For the uncut harmonic reference put \(z=g^2v/2\). Normal ordering, or the supplied one-excitation characteristic matrix, gives exactly

\[
r^{\rm ref}_0=\kappa(1-e^{-z}),\qquad
r^{\rm ref}_\alpha=\kappa[1-e^{-z}+g^2e^{-z}|\chi|^2].
\tag{5}
